object:
  tier: public-with-caveat
  consent_note: >-
    The article attributes its rhythm/celebration/gathering model to "East
    African contexts" broadly — naming saving circles, inter-clan
    coordination, and community meals after jumu'ah prayers, and later
    referencing Ramadan and harvest season as normal low-momentum periods —
    without naming a specific community or citing a community-authored
    source for these practices. This is a secondary, broad-strokes cultural
    attribution from a toolkit guide, not sourced directly from the
    communities named. Do not present as an authoritative ethnographic
    account of any specific community's practice; seek community-sourced or
    specialist review before republishing in a context that speaks on behalf
    of those traditions.
  review_type: domain-review-before-public-use
  notes: >-
    Covers the main encyclopedia entry and the "community momentum flywheel
    and rhythm model" concept-lineage entry from this work order — flagged
    per the ingest skill's TEK/cultural-attribution high-risk trigger
    (parallel to the prior gatherings-pattern.md ingestion in this
    framework).
